@database sdk.guide
@$VER: sdk.guide 1.0 (29.9.2026)

@node Main "SDK runtime payloads"
End-user components whose sources live in the zz9000-sdk
(https://github.com/BlitterStudio/zz9000-sdk) repository (where their
headers, test suite, and hardware smoke procedures are):

@{b}Payload                 | Installs to           | What it does             @{ub}
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

@{b}zz9k.library@{ub}            | @{b}LIBS:@{ub}                 | AmigaOS gateway to the   
                        |                       | SDK v2 firmware services 
                        |                       | (image/video decode,     
                        |                       | audio, compression,      
                        |                       | crypto). Requires        
                        |                       | SDK-service firmware.    

@{b}mpega.library@{ub}           | @{b}LIBS:@{ub}                 | Drop-in MPEGA replacement
                        |                       | that decodes MP3 on the  
                        |                       | ZZ9000's ARM cores;      
                        |                       | current SDK builds use   
                        |                       | host-window/card-only    
                        |                       | allocation flags for     
                        |                       | supported Zorro 2 audio  
                        |                       | buffers.                 

@{b}zz9k-picture.datatype@{ub}   | @{b}Classes/DataTypes/@{ub}    | Hardware-accelerated     
                        |                       | picture datatype;        
                        |                       | JPEG/PNG descriptors are 
                        |                       | staged inactive under    
                        |                       | @{b}Storage/DataTypes@{ub} for    
                        |                       | explicit opt-in.         

@{b}zz9k-info@{ub},              | @{b}C:@{ub}                    | Board/service            
@{b}zz9k-services@{ub}           |                       | diagnostics.             

@{b}zz9k-view@{ub}, @{b}zz9k-mp3@{ub},    | @{b}C:@{ub}                    | Feature tools:           
@{b}zz9k-cryptobench@{ub},       |                       | accelerated image viewer,
@{b}zz9k-archive@{ub}            |                       | MP3 player,              
                        |                       | crypto-offload benchmark,
                        |                       | and archive              
                        |                       | extractor/tester with    
                        |                       | LHA/LZH offload where    
                        |                       | firmware supports it.    

@{b}ZZPlay@{ub} (+ @{b}ZZPlay.info@{ub})  | @{b}SYS:Utilities/ZZ9000/@{ub} | MPEG-1/P96 video and MP3 
                        |                       | media player. Ships      
                        |                       | capitalised and with a   
                        |                       | Workbench icon, so it    
                        |                       | installs as a Workbench  
                        |                       | application rather than a
                        |                       | CLI tool, sharing the    
                        |                       | ZZ9000 drawer with ZZTop.

@{b}build.sh@{ub} pulls the SDK at the commit pinned in @{b}SDK_REF@{ub} (or uses a
@{b}ZZ9000_SDK@{ub}/sibling checkout), drives the SDK's own Docker build and package
scripts, and collects the payloads above into @{b}sdk/out/@{ub} in installer layout.
@{b}tools/package-local.sh@{ub} and the CI release assembly stage them into the
installer drawer from there.

To track a newer SDK: bump @{b}SDK_REF@{ub}, rerun @{b}sdk/build.sh@{ub}, and smoke-test per
the SDK's @{b}docs/zz9k-release-smoke.md@{ub}. Keep SDK bumps synchronized with any
driver code that consumes new SDK headers or allocation flags.

Contents

@{"Zorro II support" link zorro-ii-support}

@endnode

@node zorro-ii-support "Zorro II support"
Current matched firmware, bitstream, SDK payloads, and drivers negotiate an
aperture-relative layout before the SDK maps any host-window allocation.
The layout has two generations: generation 1 (previous matched sets, and
still published by older FPGA/firmware pairs) provides one shared 64 KiB
host heap; generation 2 (with the direct-ring reservation) shrinks the
negotiated host heap to 16 KiB on the 2 MiB and 4 MiB profiles and carves
the freed 48 KiB for the Z2 audio direct-ring grant. Both generations
negotiate and acknowledge with their own token - a driver update alone
never disables RTG on a generation-1 board. Plan Zorro II heap allocations
against the generation the card actually acknowledges (@{b}zz9k-info@{ub} reports
it).

@{b}Payload                                  | Zorro II status                 @{ub}
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

@{b}mpega.library@{ub}, @{b}mhizz9000.library@{ub}, ZZPlay | Supported on 2 MiB and 4 MiB    
standalone MP3                           | through compact staging and     
                                         | card-only rings.                

@{b}zz9k-view@{ub}, @{b}zz9k-picture.datatype@{ub}         | Supported on 2 MiB and 4 MiB    
                                         | through streamed input and      
                                         | bounded tiles. An over-wide     
                                         | DataType row rejects the        
                                         | accelerated path rather than    
                                         | overrunning the heap.           

@{b}zz9k-archive@{ub}                             | Streamed test/extract paths are 
                                         | supported; large LHA batch      
                                         | arenas retain their             
                                         | per-member/software fallback.   

Accelerated @{b}amissl.library@{ub}               | Supported on 2 MiB and 4 MiB.   
                                         | Provider open reserves one      
                                         | 32-byte probe; other exact      
                                         | 16-byte-aligned persistent      
                                         | buffers grow lazily, and a later
                                         | allocation miss falls back in   
                                         | software for that operation.    

ZZPlay MPEG-1/PIP                        | Unsupported on 2 MiB. On 4 MiB, 
                                         | one aligned YUY2 frame must fit 
                                         | the fixed 224 KiB pool; 352x288 
                                         | fits and 640x360 does not.      

Generic                                  | Still use at least one legacy   
@{b}zz9k-inflate@{ub}/@{b}zz9k-mp3@{ub}/crypto/smoke       | default shared allocation and   
diagnostics and plain @{b}zz9k-jpeg <file>@{ub}   | are not Zorro II qualification  
output                                   | tools. Use the adapted          
                                         | production clients or @{b}zz9k-jpeg 
                                         | --fb@{ub} instead.                   

The host heap is shared across clients, not provided per process. Keep the
release set matched and use @{b}zz9k-info@{ub} to verify the acknowledged layout and
memory counters. The full matrix and hardware-qualification status live in
the SDK's Zorro II service documentation
(https://github.com/BlitterStudio/zz9000-sdk/blob/master/docs/zz9k-zorro2-services.md).

